{{also|ablaté}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|ablatum}}, past participle of {{m|la|auferre||to remove}}; {{prefix|la|ab-|t1=away|ferre|t2=to carry|nocat=yes}}. First attested in the 1500s, it became obsolete by the early 1600s.<ref>{{R:CDOE|page=3}}</ref> Returned into use as a {{back-form|en|ablation|nocap=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/əˈbleɪt/|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|ablat}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[remove]] or [[decrease]] something by [[cutting]], [[erosion]], [[melting]], [[evaporation]], or [[vaporization]]. {{defdate|Late 15<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=5}}</ref>
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[undergo]] ablation; to become melted or evaporated and removed at a high temperature. {{defdate|Mid 20<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|ablator}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|ablation}}
* {{l|en|ablative}}

